ewed' means 'a greater chance of bad outcomes' — which Lottery B does not have more of than A. In fact, A has 100% chance of a loss, while B has 50% chance of a larger loss and 50% of no loss. So A assigns greater probability to a loss (100% vs 50%), meaning A is more skewed toward unfavorable outcomes. Therefore, according to the reason’s definition, A is more right-skewed. The reason would prefer A. However, this depends on whether 'unfavorable outcomes' are defined by presence of any loss or magnitude. Since A always loses money and B sometimes doesn’t, A is more consistently unfavorable. Thus, the reason would prefer A. The information is derivable, so the reason can be applied.",
